09:57:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It varies greatly by glacier, climate, altitude, and year. - In winter, glaciers gain mass mainly from **new snowfall**, which compresses into ice over time. Some summer meltwater can also refreeze within the snowpack, but most “replacement” is snowfall rather than the same melted ice freezing back. - A glacier is in balance when winter accumulation roughly equals summer melting and ice loss. - Many glaciers historically did this, gaining snow in cooler months and losing a similar amount in warmer months. - Today, most glaciers worldwide lose **more in summer than they regain in winter**, so they shrink year after year. For a healthy high-mountain glacier, winter snowfall may replace nearly all or even more than the summer loss in a snowy year. For many modern glaciers, especially at lower elevations or in warmer regions, only part of the loss is replaced—or very little during unusually warm years. Also, melted glacier water generally flows away; it does not usually freeze back onto the glacier unless it remains in cold, high-elevation snow and firn.
