09:59:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re not dumb at all. Your intuition is completely reasonable: **cold turns water into ice**, and glaciers are huge bodies of ice, so it makes sense to picture them melting in warmth and freezing again in cold. The missing piece is simply that a glacier is not like a closed pond. It is an enormous, sloping river of ice with drainage routes. Once meltwater flows away, winter cold cannot pull it back up onto the glacier to freeze there. The simple version is: - **Some** melted glacier water does freeze again when it gets cold. - **A lot** flows away before it has that chance. - Glaciers mainly rebuild through **winter snowfall** accumulating and becoming ice. - Whether a glacier grows or shrinks depends on whether winter gains outweigh summer losses. So the childhood idea was not nonsense—it captured a real part of the cycle. It just treated the glacier as if it held onto all its meltwater, like a pond does.
