09:59:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s understandable that people say it that way as a shorthand. “Glaciers melt in summer and freeze back in winter” communicates the basic idea of a seasonal ice cycle, and it is not crazy or embarrassing to have learned it that way. But for a **land glacier**, the more accurate version is: > Glaciers lose ice by melting in warm periods and gain mass mostly when winter snowfall accumulates and turns into ice. Some meltwater also refreezes before draining away. So the difference is not a nitpick about whether snow is “really water.” Snow absolutely is frozen water. The scientific issue is **mass balance**: did the water remain on the glacier, or did it drain away? A useful way to reconcile the everyday and scientific versions: - In everyday language, people may say a glacier “freezes back” in winter. - In scientific language, that usually means it **gains ice again during winter**, chiefly from snow, not that all the exact water melted in summer returns and refreezes in place. - In some places, some of the same meltwater really does refreeze—so the common picture contains a truth. - But globally, and especially for many shrinking glaciers today, the summer meltwater loss is not fully restored in winter. The earlier responses probably made the distinction sound sharper and more corrective than necessary. Your basic intuition—water freezes when it gets cold and can contribute to glacier ice—is right. The added detail is just that glaciers are open, flowing systems rather than containers that retain all their meltwater.
