10:00:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It does not have to be “plain wrong” to be a simplified idea that has been passed along. People have long observed ice and snow melting in sunlight and hardening again when temperatures drop. That happens on glaciers too, so “it melts and freezes back” is a sensible everyday description—especially from the perspective of someone visiting a mountain glacier overnight or across seasons. The part that is incomplete is treating that as the **main source of a glacier’s long-term replacement ice**. For most land glaciers: - some meltwater really does refreeze in place; - much meltwater drains downhill and leaves; - winter snowfall is the main new material that builds and sustains the glacier. A family saying can be based on a real observation without being a complete account of the whole system. For example, “the sun rises” is useful and familiar even though, scientifically, Earth’s rotation causes the apparent sunrise. So the inherited idea captures a genuine process. It just does not mean that a glacier typically restores most of its lost summer ice by retaining and refreezing that exact runoff.
