10:01:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: For the big climate-change message, you’re right: the central fact is that many glaciers are losing more ice than they gain, and that affects sea level, water supplies, ecosystems, and hazards. The snow-versus-refreezing distinction matters mainly because it tells us **why** a glacier grows or shrinks and how it will respond to warming: - If glaciers mostly recovered by refreezing their own runoff, colder winter nights might restore much of the summer loss. - In reality, much runoff leaves the glacier, so recovery depends heavily on how much winter snow falls and whether summer temperatures allow it to persist. - Warming can both increase melt and change snowfall into rain, reducing accumulation. That makes the imbalance worse. But it should not overshadow the main point. A fair plain-language summary is: > Glaciers can melt and refreeze locally, but they survive over the long term only if cold-season snow accumulation and retained refreezing make up for the ice lost to melt and runoff. So yes—the distinction is scientifically important, but your broader concern about shrinking glaciers is the more consequential issue.
