10:01:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not inconsistent—but it does mean the earlier distinction should be stated more carefully. The same basic physics applies everywhere: if liquid water remains in or near a cold environment, it can freeze and add ice. - **Sea ice:** Melted ice becomes ocean water right beneath or around the ice. In winter, that nearby seawater can freeze again. Sea ice can therefore have a strong seasonal melt–refreeze cycle. - **Cold upper parts of glaciers:** Meltwater may stay in snow, firn, pores, crevasses, or shallow ponds and refreeze. This can be an important way of retaining mass. - **Warmer/lower parts of many land glaciers:** Meltwater is more likely to drain into channels and leave as runoff before it can freeze again. There, winter snow accumulation is especially important. So the pond analogy is not “wrong for glaciers.” It applies to the fraction of meltwater that is retained. It fails only if extended to assume that *all* summer meltwater, across the whole glacier, stays available to refreeze. Also, the importance of refreezing genuinely varies. On some cold polar glaciers and ice-sheet areas, retained meltwater refreezing can be substantial. On many temperate mountain glaciers, runoff is large and snowfall is the dominant source of long-term mass gain. A balanced version is: > Glaciers can regain ice both by refreezing retained meltwater and by accumulating new snowfall. Which process matters most depends on the glacier and climate; water that drains away cannot refreeze on that glacier unless it later returns as precipitation. So your underlying logic is sound. The disagreement is not about whether glacier meltwater *can* freeze again—it clearly can—but about how much of it remains on the glacier long enough to do so.
