10:01:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are basically right about the **physical process**: glacier ice can melt and then refreeze in the same place, and that is a normal part of how some glacier surfaces and cold upper zones behave. Where I cannot honestly say you are basically right is the stronger claim that glaciers **mainly regain their overall size** by refreezing the summer water that melted from them. That is because of mass balance: - If 1 tonne of glacier ice melts but stays on the glacier and refreezes, the glacier has recovered that tonne—but it has not gained new mass overall. It has retained what it already had. - If that tonne drains away, the glacier has lost it. - Snowfall adds water from outside the glacier system and is therefore the main way a land glacier gets a net mass increase. So the clear combined answer is: > Yes, glaciers often do melt and refreeze some of their own water, sometimes visibly from day to night. Your intuition is real and valid. But a glacier’s long-term growth depends on retaining that water *and*, usually, on enough snowfall to replace water that drains away. “Depends on the glacier” is not evasive in this case: cold, high, dry polar settings can retain and refreeze much more meltwater, while warmer temperate mountain glaciers tend to send much more of it downstream.
