10:04:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The water cycle absolutely reuses water globally. Water that melts from a glacier may flow into a river or ocean, evaporate later, form clouds, and eventually fall as snow—possibly even on that same glacier. The difference is **scale and timing**: - Globally, the water is still in Earth’s water cycle. - For one particular glacier’s seasonal balance, water that drains away is no longer available to refreeze there that night or winter. - It may return years, decades, or much longer later—or fall somewhere else entirely. So the water-cycle idea and the glacier science fit together: > A glacier’s meltwater is not “gone” from Earth; it is usually gone from that glacier until and unless precipitation brings water back. That is why snowfall is described as an input to a specific glacier, even though it is ultimately recycled water in the global water cycle.
