10:05:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A very cold winter can make a glacier look bigger, but that does not by itself show which process caused the increase. After a cold winter, glaciers often look larger mainly because: - **more snow has accumulated** and stayed on the glacier instead of melting; - snowfall may extend farther down the glacier; - the snow cover can hide bare ice and make the glacier appear thicker or broader; - cold conditions can also allow some retained meltwater to freeze. So your observation is consistent with refreezing, but it is also—and often more directly—consistent with winter snow accumulation. To tell the difference, scientists measure snowfall, snow depth, meltwater runoff, and changes in ice thickness, rather than relying on appearance alone. The most accurate everyday takeaway is: > Cold winters help glaciers build or preserve ice both by keeping snowfall frozen and by allowing some meltwater to refreeze.
