10:05:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You may well have seen that phrase used. “Glacial regeneration” can be used in popular writing or in specific projects to mean a glacier or ice mass rebuilding, recovering, or retaining ice—including through refreezing. But it is not the usual precise term glaciologists use for the annual process. They would typically distinguish: - **refreezing / meltwater retention** — meltwater freezes within snow, firn, or ice; - **accumulation** — snow and other frozen precipitation add mass; - **mass balance** — the total gains versus melting, runoff, and calving losses. So the article’s wording supports the ordinary idea that refreezing can help a glacier recover or preserve ice. It does not necessarily mean that refreezing of its own summer runoff is the main source of regeneration for glaciers generally. A fair way to put your point is: > Glaciers can regenerate some ice by retaining and refreezing meltwater, while winter snow accumulation is also a major part of how they rebuild and persist. That is not a contradiction—both are real parts of the system.
